#errorCatcher Echo
#encoding UTF-8
## Every English string is a $gettext key (the key IS the English text, so
## a missing [Texts] entry falls back to it); the report's lang file
## translates the page.  tests/test_celestial.py reads these keys from
## this file and from celestial_page.py (the strings the javascript
## composes) and enforces that lang/en.conf ships exactly the keys that
## render.
## time and datetime dress the header's "updated" stamp (see there).
#import time
#import datetime
## $panels is $celestial, the page's panels (celestial_page.py's
## CelestialPage), resolved ONCE here and tested at every call below:
## a weewx.conf stanza overriding search_list_extensions with the
## pre-9.0 shim leaves $celestial unbound while $sky_page still
## resolves, and under #errorCatcher Echo a bare placeholder would
## then ECHO its own text into the page; $getVar's default makes it
## a page without the panels, which is what upgrading.md says that
## override costs.  Render-time failures need no guard here: every
## panel method wears celestial_page's _panel_guard and renders
## blank, logged, on its own.
#set $panels = $getVar('celestial', None)
## The page's plate: the report's `theme` option (dark | light | auto,
## default dark), resolved at generation by $celestial.theme_class --
## through weewx-skyfield's SkyPage, so the option is spelled and valued
## exactly as skyfield's own Sky page spells it and `auto` is skyfield's
## sunrise logic, not a second copy.  Resolved at generation because the
## dome and the Next Visible Pass chart arrive as skyfield SVG with
## their colors inside them and the javascript refetches them every
## 60 s, so a browser-side toggle could not move them and the next
## refetch would undo it anyway; the dome and pass panels resolve their
## own plate from the same site, so the two cannot disagree.  Dark
## without $panels.
#set $theme_class = 'theme-dark'
#if $panels
#set $theme_class = $panels.theme_class($almanac)
#end if
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="$lang" class="$theme_class">
  <head>
    <meta http-equiv="Content-Type" content="text/html; charset=utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    #if $Extras.has_key('meta_title')
      <title>$Extras.meta_title</title>
    #else
      <title>$station.location</title>
    #end if
    <meta name="description" content="$gettext('The live sky over {location}.').format(location=$station.location)">
    ## All styling lives in celestial.css: Cheetah owns '#', so hex colors
    ## must never appear in this template (celestial.js carries none
    ## either: the stylesheet owns every color, as a token set a consumer
    ## can restyle).  The skin version tags the three URLs so browsers
    ## refetch the files after an upgrade (CopyGenerator re-copies
    ## copy_once files on every report first-run, but a browser would
    ## happily keep serving its cached copy of an unversioned URL).
    #set $vtag = ''
    #if $Extras.has_key('version')
    #set $vtag = '?v=' + str($Extras.version)
    #end if
    ## Two sheets since 9.0: celestial.css is the PANELS, which a skin
    ## embedding them loads on its own page, and celestial-page.css is
    ## this report's own surfaces and chrome -- the body, header, h1,
    ## section card and footer.  A consumer must not load the second:
    ## those rules name bare elements and would repaint its whole site.
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="celestial.css$vtag">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="celestial-page.css$vtag">
    ## The page's javascript, celestial.js: one static file (CopyGenerator
    ## copy_once, like sky.js), started by the config block at the top of
    ## <body>.  Deliberately NOT deferred: start() arms the loop poll, and
    ## it must be armed before the panels below parse so a first packet
    ## can land while the page is still streaming in -- deferred, it would
    ## run only after parsing, and the parse-time paths the javascript
    ## keeps for that case (a packet or a backdrop refetch owed while the
    ## document is still loading) would never run.
    <script src="celestial.js$vtag"></script>
    ## Tap tooltips (sky.js, copied verbatim from weewx-skyfield): SVG
    ## <title> tooltips are hover-only, dead on touch; a tap shows the
    ## same text as a floating chip.
    <script src="sky.js$vtag" defer></script>
  </head>
  <body>
    ## The live layer starts here, before any panel has parsed: the
    ## config block $celestial builds from this report -- its [Extras],
    ## the station, this almanac's instant and names, the report's units,
    ## [Texts], satellites and comets -- and the celestial.start call.
    ## Everything the javascript needs per report is in that block; the
    ## script itself is static.  $filename is core's: this page's own
    ## path under HTML_ROOT, from which the block carries the route up
    ## to HTML_ROOT, where the fragments are written.  (Through 8.5 this was
    ## `#include "realtime_updater.inc"`, the whole script with those
    ## values baked in.)
    #if $panels
    $panels.config_script($almanac, $filename)
    #end if
    ## Station coordinates, hemisphere-lettered for the header.  The
    ## letters are $station.latitude/longitude[2]: core WeeWX computes
    ## them from the report's [Labels] hemispheres (lang-file aware).
    ## There is NO $Labels tag in the core search list -- 7.2 referenced
    ## one and every production page died with "cannot find 'Labels'".
    #set $lat_f = $station.stn_info.latitude_f
    #set $lon_f = $station.stn_info.longitude_f
    #set $lat_letter = $station.latitude[2]
    #set $lon_letter = $station.longitude[2]
    <div class="cel-page">
      <header>
        <div>
          <h1>
            #if $Extras.has_key('title')
              $Extras.title
            #else
              $gettext('Celestial <span class="over">over</span> {location}').format(location=$station.location)
            #end if
          </h1>
          <div class="cel-sub cel-mono">
            $("%.2f&deg; %s &middot; %.2f&deg; %s" % (abs($lat_f), $lat_letter, abs($lon_f), $lon_letter))
            ## The "updated" stamp first-paints the generation instant -- the
            ## page's clock until the first loop packet (the config block's
            ## gen_ts) -- in the shape fmtHMS repaints it in: %H:%M:%S.
            ## And in the ZONE fmtHMS repaints it in, which is the report's
            ## own time_zone Extras setting where it names one, NOT simply
            ## weewxd's: a station displaying another zone would otherwise
            ## paint station-local here and have the first packet rewrite
            ## it, a visible jump in the most prominent spot on the page.
            ## Two cases no generation-time value can match, because the
            ## zone the javascript will use is the VIEWER's and there is no
            ## viewer yet: time_zone = browser, and a zone name the
            ## javascript's own probe rejects (a misspelled IANA name,
            ## which it logs and then falls back to browser-local for).  In
            ## both the stamp stays station-local and the first packet
            ## moves it.  That is the right trade rather than a defect to
            ## design around: a report generates a COMPLETE page, so the
            ## first paint must be a real instant in a defined zone, not a
            ## blank waiting for a packet that may never come.
            ## The #try also covers a python too old for zoneinfo; the
            ## fallback costs the zone and never the page.
            #set $gen_hms = time.strftime('%H:%M:%S', time.localtime($almanac.time_ts))
            #if $Extras.has_key('time_zone') and str($Extras.time_zone) != 'browser'
            #try
            #set $gen_hms = datetime.datetime.fromtimestamp($almanac.time_ts, __import__('zoneinfo').ZoneInfo(str($Extras.time_zone))).strftime('%H:%M:%S')
            #except
            #pass
            #end try
            #end if
            &middot; $gettext("updated") <span id="last-update">$gen_hms</span> <span id="live-label"></span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </header>

      ## Countdown central: the row of countdown chips, first-painted
      ## from this almanac and re-rendered by celestial.js on every loop
      ## packet -- $celestial.countdown_html owns the row, the chip
      ## rulings and their first paints.
      #if $panels
      $panels.countdown_html($almanac)
      #end if

      <section>
        <h2 class="cel-eyebrow">$gettext("The geocentric · live")</h2>
        ## The Geocentric: the dial (javascript-built on the first loop
        ## packet) and the roster, first-painted from this almanac and
        ## kept live by celestial.js -- $celestial.geocentric_html, which
        ## carries the panel's install hint when no extended almanac
        ## serves it.
        #if $panels
        $panels.geocentric_html($almanac)
        #end if
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="cel-eyebrow">$gettext("The sky dome · live")</h2>
        ## The dome -- weewx-skyfield's dome_svg for the page's fragment
        ## set (the default: no [CelestialFragments] section, so
        ## dome-svg*.txt at scale 1.0 on the report's theme) with its
        ## self-describing wrapper, caption and health line, or the
        ## install hint when the almanac cannot draw it -- and beside it
        ## the any-pass satellite roster.  The grid that puts the two side
        ## by side is this page's chrome; the panels are $celestial's.
        #if $panels
        <div class="cel-domepanel">
          $panels.dome_html($almanac)
          $panels.dome_roster_html($almanac)
        </div>
        #end if
      </section>

      ## The Next Visible Pass panel: the chart (skyfield's
      ## pass_chart_html for the page's set) beside the visible-pass
      ## roster, the chart's own story.  The chart area hides itself when
      ## no configured satellite has a visible pass in its elements'
      ## validity window; this section hides too when the roster is empty
      ## as well -- pass_panel_hidden is the same chart and rows -- and
      ## the javascript unhides it (by this id) when a pass enters the
      ## window.  No panel at all without a sky page: nothing to refetch.
      #if $panels
      #set $pass_html = $panels.pass_html($almanac)
      #if $pass_html
      #set $pass_sec_attr = ' hidden' if $panels.pass_panel_hidden($almanac) else ''
      <section id="pass-sec"$pass_sec_attr>
        <h2 class="cel-eyebrow">$gettext("The Next Visible Pass · the sky at its peak")</h2>
        <div class="cel-passpanel">
          $pass_html
          $panels.pass_roster_html($almanac)
        </div>
      </section>
      #end if
      #end if

      <footer>
        ## The credit, true for whatever almanac actually serves the page
        ## ($celestial.footer_html probes it), closed by the loopdata one.
        #if $panels
        $panels.footer_html($almanac)
        #end if
      </footer>
    </div>
  </body>
</html>
